// PIN (RED — waiting on a spelling, not on a fix): the stdin twin of 335_042.
// `std/io:read-stdin` allocates with `koru_allocator()` (io.kz:157,
// `readToEndAlloc`) and returns a bare `| ok string` (io.kz:151). The caller
// prints it and drops it; the buffer lives until process exit and no pass says
// anything, because the payload carries no phantom.
//
// The pair matters because it shows the hole is the SURFACE, not one tor:
// every `readToEndAlloc` path in io.kz (read-stdin:157, read-file:189,
// read-line:129) hands back an allocation the obligation system cannot see.
//
// The spelling question lives in 335_042's header — the label, its disposer, and
// whether one label covers the whole IO read surface. Bring it; do not invent it.
//
// Grounding (verified 2026-07-31):
//   read-stdin decl  — koru_std/io.kz:150, `| ok string` at 151
//   the allocation   — koru_std/io.kz:157

~import std/io

// Read all of stdin, use the content, drop it — no disposer exists to call.
~std/io:read-stdin()
| ok content |> std/io:print.ln("{{ content:s }}")
| eof |> _
| failed _ |> _
